      "obligation": "refuter:6:1",
      "id": "cor-lyapunov-moment-inequality-on-a-probability-space",
      "location": "Facts & Assumptions [L2] and step 1.2",
      "defect": "citation-inaccurate",
      "evidence": "The item permits p=q=infinity, then says that the q=infinity case follows by the same specialization of [L2]. The cited dependency states its L-infinity inclusion only for “1<=p<infinity,” whereas step 1.2 invokes it without that restriction and writes the factor P(Omega)^(1/p). Thus the citation does not cover the allowed p=q=infinity endpoint. That endpoint is trivially true, but it is not licensed by the cited result.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "84e8fa38d14c7f5f1218056160c9274809fd8dec40694d10674f0ed532223d62"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:6:2",
      "id": "ex-discrete-continuous-and-mixed-distribution-functions",
      "location": "Facts & Assumptions [L1] and step 1.1",
      "defect": "missing-hypothesis",
      "evidence": "The cited theorem begins its Statement with “Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice” before giving the converse from a distribution function to a probability law. This example assumes only the three displayed functions, while [L1] unconditionally says “Distribution functions determine probability laws” and step 1.1 invokes it. The required Countable Choice hypothesis is absent.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "3f0e4eefa32e426abb9a16d82b5441f648e4c1382e8e8ffa481d0d3c7271585f"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:6:3",
      "id": "ex-expectation-under-a-measurable-transformation",
      "location": "Example",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "The Example and step 1.1 claim the weighted expectation is 2, but the displayed arithmetic is 2/4 + 1/2 + 5/4 = 1/2 + 1/2 + 5/4 = 9/4. Hence E[g(X)] is 9/4, not 2.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "1db0dd1b815c6983241507d4939671f54bc073d3fffae505212aca06b15d13ed"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:6:4",
      "id": "ex-exponential-random-variable-from-its-tail",
      "location": "Facts & Assumptions [L2] and step 2.1",
      "defect": "missing-hypothesis",
      "evidence": "The cited probability-law/distribution-function theorem begins “Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice.” This example has no such assumption, but [L2] says unconditionally that distribution functions determine probability laws and step 2.1 uses that assertion to identify the law uniquely. The cited dependency therefore does not license the inference as written.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "48c160a84492b5e3728aa79ddfc1400450327b01e9c067def421ed16e0b9ac54"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:6:5",
      "id": "ex-jensen-for-logarithm-and-exponential",
      "location": "Example, clause 1 and Verification step 1.2",
      "defect": "missing-hypothesis",
      "evidence": "The cited Jensen theorem requires its input random variable to be integrable. The example assumes only that X is positive and integrable, then applies Jensen to Z=log X. On Omega=N with P({n})=6/(pi^2 n^2) and X(n)=exp(-n^2), X is positive and integrable, but E[|log X|]=infinity, so log X is not integrable and the displayed E[log X] is not an expectation under the library definition. An integrability condition on log X (or an explicitly defined extended-expectation formulation) is required.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "b9244530a31cc8d4fbca7e9418c06853cf6959719946c149a8e1ee3158256d18"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:6:6",
      "id": "rem-general-inequalities-restrict-to-the-published-finite-ones",
      "location": "Remark, fourth bullet",
      "defect": "overstrong-title-or-statement",
      "evidence": "The Remark says cor-second-moment-positive-probability-bound “restricts to” thm-finite-second-moment-lower-bound. The corollary assumes X is nonnegative and concludes a bound for P(X>0); the cited finite theorem applies to every finite real random variable and bounds P(X≠0). A finite Rademacher variable X in {-1,1} satisfies the finite theorem but not the corollary's nonnegativity hypothesis. Thus the general result does not restrict to the full published finite statement.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "13218b3ab575fed597bc632185ce442b3b8f79e52a2f3fcd45bfaa21925cf6e1"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:6:7",
      "id": "thm-normal-equations-for-best-affine-l2-prediction",
      "location": "Statement",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "The Statement says “Every representative of that minimizing class is an affine predictor.” The cited Lp definition says an element is an almost-everywhere equivalence class of measurable representatives. On ([0,1],B,lambda), let X=0 and Y_1=0. The unique minimizing class is [0], but 1_{ {0} } is also a representative of [0] and is not pointwise of the form a_0+a_1Y_1, since every such expression is constant. Only existence of an affine representative is true.",
